Julius for RISC OS
==================

Julius is an open-source version of Caesar III, the Roman city-building
game by Impressions Games (1998). It plays exactly like the original and
reads and writes the same saved games, with a few improvements: any
window size, wider screens, and many small interface conveniences.

  Julius:      https://github.com/bvschaik/julius
  This port:   https://github.com/adyoull/riscos-julius

Version: see docs.Version.

You need the original game
--------------------------
Julius uses the pictures, sounds, films and scenarios of the original
Caesar III, version 1.0.1.0 (CD, GOG or Steam). They aren't included.
Copy them into !Julius.C3data: C3data.ReadMe says which files and how to
get them off the CD. Or keep them elsewhere and *Set Julius$Data to
their directory.

What you need
-------------
- RISC OS 5 on an ARMv7 machine with VFP (Raspberry Pi 2 or later,
  BeagleBoard, Titanium, ...). A Pi 4 is recommended.
- The ARMEABISupport module and SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later (from
  !PackMan) in !System.
- For sound: SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager, in ssb.zip on the
  RDPClient page: https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html
  John Duffell's own site (now on the Internet Archive) has more details:
  https://web.archive.org/web/20110920080106/http://www.duffell.riscos.me.uk/
  Merge ssb.zip's !System with yours. SharedSound is part of RISC OS.
  Without them the game is silent.
- About 60 MB of free memory, plus 16 MB for the program.

Playing
-------
Double-click !Julius. The game opens full screen (a desktop window that
covers the screen, so other programs keep running). F6 or Alt-Enter
switch between a window and full screen.
The arrow keys scroll the map.

Settings and saved games are kept in the data folder (C3data).

In !Run you can:
- set Julius$FPS: frames drawn per second (default 50; 0 = no limit),
- turn on a log (<Wimp$ScrapDir>.juliuslog) to send with a report.

Problems
--------
Report them at https://github.com/adyoull/riscos-julius/issues with the
log (see !Run).

